ation. I need to be more strategic and patient.\n\nTherefore, the best play is to pass. This saves the Red Joker for a more critical moment and ensures the opponents have to lead, giving me information about their hands.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My current hand consists of single cards (6, 10, 2, Red Joker) and pairs (JJ, KK). While I hold the highest card (Red Joker) and a high card (2), the rest of my hand (6, 10, JJ, KK) is not connected enough to "run" the table immediately. I have weak singles (6, 10) that are difficult to dispose of without a free turn or a low lead from opponents. Beating the ♠2 with the Red Joker would consume my ultimate control card and leave me with a hand that still requires 4-5 turns to empty, likely losing the lead to the Black Joker, Bombs, or Aces/2s held by the opponents (who still have 16 cards each).\n\nReason: It is not strategic to use the Red Joker to beat a single 2 when I cannot guarantee clearing my hand afterwards. The Red Joker is best saved to beat the Black Joker or to regain control at a critical moment when my hand size is smaller or when I can force a win. By passing, I keep the Red Joker and 2 as a strong defensive backbone. I hope for the opponent to lead a pair (for my JJ/KK) or a low single (allowing me to clear trash or use face cards), or for the opponents to fight amongst themselves.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
